# cppcheck per-site suppressions — INVARIANT-13.

# Format: <id>:<file>:<line>.  Blank lines and `#` comments are ignored.

# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# --------------------
# The static-analysis workflow used to pass four FILE-WIDE suppressions on the
# command line:

#   --suppress=uninitvar:src/c/ama_nistp.c
#   --suppress=uninitvar:src/c/ama_kyber.c
#   --suppress=uninitvar:src/c/ama_dilithium.c
#   --suppress=arrayIndexOutOfBounds:src/c/dispatch/ama_dispatch.c

# Each was justified in a comment, and each silenced the whole ID for the whole
# file: a genuinely uninitialised read introduced later into ama_nistp.c — 1,100
# lines of P-256/P-384/P-521 field arithmetic — would have been reported and
# discarded.  ama_dispatch.c was the substantive one, being full of
# `char[AMA_DISPATCH_PATH_MAX]` buffers.

# INVARIANT-13 forbids in-source suppression markers under src/c/ regardless of
# justification, so `--inline-suppr` comments are not the alternative.  A
# suppressions file is: it is per site, it carries the reason, and it lives
# outside the sources.

# Line numbers drift.  That is the intended failure mode: a suppression whose
# line moved stops matching, the finding reappears, and CI goes red until
# someone confirms it is still the same false positive.  A file-wide
# suppression can never do that.

# arrayIndexOutOfBounds is NOT listed here.  It was not a cppcheck limitation
# at all: with `--force`, cppcheck invents a configuration in which PATH_MAX is
# defined but unknown, evaluates `char dir[AMA_DISPATCH_PATH_MAX]` as `dir[1]`,
# and reports the writes into it.  Passing the real value, `-DPATH_MAX=4096`,
# removes both findings outright and leaves the check LIVE on those buffers,
# which is strictly better than silencing it.  Verified with cppcheck 2.13.0:
# the two findings at ama_dispatch.c:1010 and :1015 disappear and no new
# finding appears anywhere.

# ama_nistp.c — `out` is a local ge/fe struct passed by address to
# nistp_mont_mul / nistp_mod_sub, which write every limb before any read.
# cppcheck does not follow through those helpers, so it sees `*r = out` as a
# read of an uninitialised object.
uninitvar:src/c/ama_nistp.c:699
uninitvar:src/c/ama_nistp.c:801
uninitvar:src/c/ama_nistp.c:1168

# ama_kyber.c — `&mat[i].vec[j]` takes the address of a matrix entry so
# kyber_gen_matrix can fill it.  Taking the address of an object in order to
# have it written is the operation cppcheck cannot distinguish from reading it.
uninitvar:src/c/ama_kyber.c:577

# ama_dilithium.c — w1_packed is filled by polyveck_pack_w1 through a pointer.
uninitvar:src/c/ama_dilithium.c:2363

# ama_ed25519_ge.h — the group-arithmetic template.  recip / u / v / w / v3 are
# GE_FE locals passed by address to fe_invert_ct and ge_decode_prepare, which
# write every limb before any read.  With --force cppcheck invents a config in
# which GE_SUFFIX is defined and then cannot follow the GE_SYM()-named helpers,
# so it reads the address-of as a read of an uninitialised object — the same
# out-parameter false positive as the ama_nistp / ama_kyber / ama_dilithium
# entries above.
uninitvar:src/c/internal/ama_ed25519_ge.h:506
uninitvar:src/c/internal/ama_ed25519_ge.h:591
uninitvar:src/c/internal/ama_ed25519_ge.h:602
uninitvar:src/c/internal/ama_ed25519_ge.h:603

# ama_fe25519_safegcd.h — `c1 = (uint64_t)(zeta >> 63)` broadcasts the sign bit
# of the signed zeta as an all-ones / all-zeros mask, the constant-time idiom
# this codebase uses throughout (see the shiftTooManyBitsSigned note in
# static-analysis.yml: arithmetic right shift on every supported target, result
# cast straight to uint64_t and used only as a mask).
shiftNegativeLHS:src/c/internal/ama_fe25519_safegcd.h:124
